{"name":"Sweetest Damask","hex":"#a7bdba","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sweetest","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["superlative form of sweet: most sweet"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sweetest"},{"term":"damask","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An ornate silk fabric originating from Damascus.","Linen so woven that a pattern is produced by the different directions of the thread, without contrast of colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a grayish-pink colour, like that of the damask rose."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/damask"}],"entries":[{"title":"Damask","blocks":[{"text":"Damask is a woven, reversible patterned fabric. Damasks are woven by periodically reversing the action of the warp and weft threads. The pattern is most commonly created with a warp-faced satin weave and the ground with a weft-faced or sateen weave."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/ItalianSilkDamask.jpg/330px-ItalianSilkDamask.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damask","wikidata":"Q901382"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/ItalianSilkDamask.jpg/500px-ItalianSilkDamask.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/ItalianSilkDamask.jpg/500px-ItalianSilkDamask.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Italian silk polychrome damasks, 14th century","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AItalianSilkDamask.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Damask_with_floral_sprigs%2C_Italy%2C_Baroque%2C_1600-1650%2C_silk_two-tone_damask_-_Royal_Ontario_Museum_-_DSC04376.JPG/500px-Damask_with_floral_sprigs%2C_Italy%2C_Baroque%2C_1600-1650%2C_silk_two-tone_damask_-_Royal_Ontario